(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 74-year-old man presents with a two-month history of intense itching followed by blistering. Examination shows numerous 1 to 3 cm tense, firm bullae on the trunk, flexural surfaces of the arms, and the axillary and inguinal folds. Rubbing the skin adjacent to a blister does not cause the epidermis to slough. There is a single small erosion in the mouth. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythema multiforme","Bullous impetigo","Bullous pemphigoid","Pemphigus vulgaris","Dermatitis herpetiformis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bullous pemphigoid damages the epithelial basement membrane, producing subepithelial blistering, and classically presents after age 60 with tense 1 to 3 cm bullae on the trunk, extremity flexures, and axillary and inguinal folds. A pruritic prodrome may precede the bullae by weeks to months, and Nikolsky's sign is negative. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 5-month-old girl has a large hemangioma of the face lying close to the eye and the corner of the mouth. What complications should be considered?","choices":["Malignant transformation to angiosarcoma","Compression of vital structures","Progressive darkening throughout life","No complications occur","Systemic hypertension from shunting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemangiomas may compress vital structures, block vision, interfere with feeding or respiration, obstruct the external auditory canal, and occur at extracutaneous sites. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has several tense, clear-to-cloudy blisters on the trunk arising on intact skin. Some have ruptured, leaving shallow moist erosions ringed by a collarette of scale. There is no lymphadenopathy. What organism and mechanism are responsible?","choices":["Corynebacterium in the stratum corneum","Staphylococcus aureus alone, via epidermolytic toxin","Streptococcus pyogenes, by dermal invasion","Autoantibody against the basement membrane","Staphylococcus or Streptococcus, via a skin break"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bullous impetigo is caused exclusively by Staphylococcus aureus, which releases epidermolytic toxins producing epidermal splitting. Bullae occur on intact skin, rupture to leave collarettes, and lymphadenopathy is uncommon — in contrast to non-bullous disease, where it is common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with tinea corporis asks whether the infection can spread to her mouth, and a student asks why dermatophytes never involve mucous membranes. What is the correct explanation?","choices":["They require sebum","They are obligate intracellular organisms","They survive only on dead keratin","They can involve mucosa if immunocompromised","Salivary enzymes kill them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dermatophytes infect and survive only on dead keratin, involving the superficial portion of the skin at the stratum corneum, plus hair and nails. They cannot survive on mucous membranes. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 36-year-old pregnant woman and her 7-year-old son both develop erythema migrans after a camping trip. What is first-line oral antibiotic therapy for Lyme disease, and what alternative applies to children and pregnant women with early disease?","choices":["Doxycycline, or amoxicillin in pregnancy","Topical permethrin to the bite","Intravenous ceftriaxone for all stages","Amoxicillin, with doxycycline for failure","No antibiotics for stage 1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are indicated in all stages. Doxycycline is first line, with amoxicillin as an alternative first-line agent for early disease including erythema migrans, particularly in children and pregnant women. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy is brought in with fever and a rash. Examination shows non-blanching deposits of blood in the skin, several measuring 6 mm across. What is the most appropriate interpretation of this finding?","choices":["Purpura, benign and observable","A wheal from dermal fluid","Ecchymosis from minor trauma","Petechiae, an emergency until proven otherwise","Purpura, an emergency until proven otherwise"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Purpura is a deposit of blood 4 mm or greater, and the lecture flags it explicitly as a medical emergency until proven otherwise. Combined with fever in a child this demands immediate evaluation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has symmetrical, well-defined white non-scaly macules and patches on the dorsal hands and around the mouth. They fluoresce under a Wood's lamp. What is the underlying mechanism?","choices":["Proliferation of basal melanocytes with increased melanin","T cell mediated autoimmune destruction of melanocytes","Postinflammatory loss of pigment after a dermatitis","Overgrowth of Malassezia altering melanocyte function","Reduced melanin production by intact melanocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vitiligo is a common autoimmune disease causing depigmentation through T cell mediated destruction of melanocytes. It can begin at any age but usually starts before the thirties, with half of patients presenting before their twenties. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with diabetes presents with an extremely painful lesion on the back of the neck. Examination shows a deep, indurated plaque with several separate openings draining pus. He has malaise, chills, and a fever of 38.4 degrees Celsius. What is the lesion?","choices":["Folliculitis","A cutaneous abscess","A carbuncle","Hidradenitis suppurativa","A furuncle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A carbuncle is two or more confluent furuncles with separate heads, arising in several hair follicles, and systemic symptoms are more common with a carbuncle than a furuncle. Incision and drainage is the mainstay, supported by oral antibiotics. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has a circumscribed light brown patch on the trunk resembling a café au lait spot, within which several darker pigmented macules and papules are visible. What is this lesion?","choices":["A congenital melanocytic naevus","A dysplastic naevus","A cafe au lait macule","Nevus spilus","A blue nevus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nevus spilus, the spotted nevus, is a variant of congenital naevus present at birth or in the first years of life, most commonly on the trunk and extremities. Darkly pigmented macules or papules sit within a background that is circumscribed and similar in appearance to a café au lait spot, and it rarely progresses to melanoma. Management is observation, periodic evaluation, and sun protection. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man is found on routine examination to have several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on the trunk that blanch with pressure. He has never noticed them and they are asymptomatic. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cherry angioma","Melanocytic naevus","Pyogenic granuloma","Nevus araneus","Petechiae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cherry angiomas are acquired vascular lesions formed by capillary proliferation, very common and increasing with age, appearing as smooth firm deep red papules under 5 mm on the trunk that blanch with pressure. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl is diagnosed with atopic dermatitis and a student asks how common the condition is and who it affects. What statement is most accurate?","choices":["About 20 percent; never begins after childhood","More common in females than males","About 2 percent of children","It resolves in all by adolescence","About 20 percent of children; often begins early"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Atopic dermatitis affects 20 percent of children worldwide and often begins in infancy or childhood. It is more common in males than females, may improve, persist, or recur, and adult-onset disease also occurs though it is rare. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man presents with an engorged tick attached to his flank after a hike in an endemic area. What single action is emphasised first in the management of a tick-borne illness such as Lyme disease?","choices":["Administer prophylactic intravenous antibiotics to all patients","Remove the tick immediately","Send the tick for species identification before any treatment","Await the appearance of erythema migrans before acting","Apply a topical corticosteroid to the bite site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The management sequence begins with removing the tick immediately, followed by antibiotics, which are indicated in all stages. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has an intensely pruritic eruption of violaceous flat-topped papules on both wrists, and a biopsy is taken from one lesion. What histopathological finding is most characteristic?","choices":["Granulomas around degenerated collagen","Acantholysis with intraepithelial blisters","Parakeratosis with neutrophilic microabscesses","A band-like lymphocytic infiltrate","Subepithelial blistering with neutrophils"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy in lichen planus shows band-like infiltration of lymphocytes in the dermis. Additional characteristic findings include hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis, vacuolisation of the basal layer, Civatte bodies in the lower epidermis, and saw-tooth shaped rete ridges. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 177"},{"q":"A 29-year-old hairdresser reports recurrent episodes of intensely itchy blisters on her hands. Examination shows multiple grouped 1 to 2 mm vesicles on the lateral aspects of the fingers and the adjacent interdigital surfaces, some coalescing, on a poorly defined pink base with surrounding dryness. She reports previous episodes that persisted for several weeks, dried up, and then peeled. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis of the hands","Allergic contact dermatitis","Irritant contact dermatitis","Dyshidrotic eczema","Nummular eczema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dyshidrotic eczema is a chronic, relapsing eruption of intensely pruritic vesicles on the hands and feet, classically described as tapioca vesicles. They may coalesce into bullae, persist for several weeks, then desiccate and resolve with desquamation — the exact course she describes. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman is prescribed topical permethrin for scabies. What counselling should be given about the response to treatment?","choices":["Overnight to all skin; persistent itch means failure","Overnight to all skin; itch may persist for weeks","Weekly for three months","To visible areas only; resolves in 24 hours","Twenty minutes then rinse; no other measures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Topical permethrin is applied overnight to the entire skin surface with special attention to creases. Most patients experience relief in about 3 days, but rash and pruritus may persist afterwards, which patients must be warned about or they will assume treatment failure. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with erythema multiforme triggered by recurrent herpes simplex virus has had four episodes in twelve months. What management addresses the recurrences?","choices":["Continuous oral antihistamines","Suppressive oral acyclovir or valacyclovir","Continuous topical corticosteroids","Avoid anti-inflammatories and sulfonamides","Oral prednisone at each episode"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral acyclovir or valacyclovir is used when the eruption is triggered by herpes simplex virus, and a prior episode is itself a risk factor for recurrence. Suppressing the trigger is what prevents the next episode. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl presents with comedones, papules, and pustules of the face and upper back. What are the four factors involved in the pathogenesis of acne vulgaris?","choices":["Apocrine occlusion, bacteria, sinus tracts, scarring","Hyperkeratinisation, reduced sebum, bacteria, inflammation","Hyperkeratinisation, sebum, Cutibacterium, inflammation","Autoimmune folliculitis and androgen excess","Neurovascular dysregulation and Demodex overgrowth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acne involves follicular hyperkeratinisation with excess keratin in the follicle, increased sebum production providing a growth medium, proliferation of Cutibacterium acnes, and inflammation. The temporal sequence among them is not fully understood. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has a bright red, friable, pedunculated papule on the finger pad that appeared rapidly after she injured the finger and bleeds easily when bumped. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Verruca vulgaris","Pyogenic granuloma","Dermatofibroma","Cherry angioma","Amelanotic melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pyogenic granuloma is an acquired benign vascular tumour arising as a response to injury or hormonal factors, common in children, young adults, and pregnancy, most often on the head, neck, and fingers, and it bleeds readily. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a lifetime of outdoor work has multiple rough, scaly erythematous papules on the scalp and dorsal hands, each 2 to 8 mm, that feel like sandpaper when palpated. What molecular event is the critical step in their development?","choices":["Ultraviolet-induced TP53 mutation","Loss of elastic tissue","Ultraviolet-induced melanocyte proliferation","Hyperinsulinaemia driving keratinocyte growth","Immunoglobulin A complex deposition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses are intraepidermal keratinocytic dysplasias induced by cumulative ultraviolet B exposure, and ultraviolet-induced mutations in TP53 are the critical molecular event. They represent a field cancerisation process. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has confirmed dermatophyte onychomycosis of two fingernails and four toenails. What is the usual duration of first-line oral terbinafine for each?","choices":["Twelve weeks fingernails, six weeks toenails","Six weeks for both","Six weeks fingernails, twelve weeks toenails","Two weeks for both","Lifelong therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oral terbinafine is first-line for most dermatophyte nail disease, usually 6 weeks for fingernails and 12 weeks for toenails, with hepatic disease and interactions reviewed and baseline liver tests obtained per labelling and patient risk. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 38-year-old farmer has tender, boggy, pustular plaques in the beard area. Hairs in the affected area are easily removed. What therapy is required?","choices":["A change of shaving technique","A topical retinoid","Oral antifungal therapy","Oral cephalexin","A topical antifungal cream alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hair is easily removed in tinea barbae unlike in bacterial folliculitis, and a bacterial culture can be done to rule the latter out. Topical antifungals do not penetrate the hair follicle, so oral therapy with griseofulvin or terbinafine is required. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has hypopigmented scaly macules over the upper back and shoulders confirmed as pityriasis versicolor. What is first-line treatment, and when is systemic therapy appropriate?","choices":["Topical first line; oral if refractory","A high-potency topical corticosteroid","Topical nystatin first line","Oral fluconazole for everyone","Oral terbinafine first line"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical therapy is first line, including ketoconazole shampoo or cream, selenium sulfide, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox, or topical terbinafine. Systemic therapy with oral fluconazole or itraconazole is reserved for extensive, recurrent, or topical-refractory disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a lifetime of outdoor work has multiple 0.3 to 0.5 cm pink papules on the scalp and dorsal hands. Several are more apparent to touch than to sight. One lesion is thick, indurated, and has begun to bleed. What does that single lesion most require?","choices":["Observation for one year","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy","Field-directed therapy","Reassurance","Biopsy"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The most important distinction for actinic keratosis is early squamous cell carcinoma or carcinoma in situ, especially when a lesion is thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent, or recurrent. Those features change the lesion from one to treat destructively to one to sample. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 16-year-old football player develops acne confined to the shoulders and upper back under his pads. What predisposing factor does this represent?","choices":["Emotional stress raising cortisol","Drug-induced acne from an anabolic steroid","Endocrine acne from insulin resistance","Acne mechanica from pressure on the follicle","Genetic predisposition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acne mechanica results from pressure on the skin — shoulder pads, orthopaedic casts, and helmets are the named examples — occluding the pilosebaceous follicle. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has a painful fluctuant nodule 4 mm across on the back of the neck. He is afebrile and it is a single lesion. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Squeezing the lesion","Immediate incision and drainage","Oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Warm compresses plus oral cephalexin","Warm compresses without antibiotics"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Warm compresses are usually sufficient for small furuncles, and no antibiotics are given if the patient is afebrile with a single lesion under 5 mm. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 19-year-old presents in summer with numerous well-defined hypopigmented macules over the upper back and shoulders that do not tan. Scraping the surface reveals fine scale. What does potassium hydroxide microscopy show?","choices":["Budding yeast with pseudohyphae","No organisms; biopsy is needed","Short hyphae with yeast clusters","Long septate branching hyphae","Mites, eggs, and fecal pellets"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Potassium hydroxide microscopy in pityriasis versicolor shows short hyphae with clusters of yeast, classically described as spaghetti and meatballs. The condition is caused by overgrowth of lipid-dependent Malassezia species that normally inhabit the skin. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman presents with a change in one fingernail and arrives wearing dark polish on every nail. What examination step is specified when evaluating a nail for a possible neoplasm?","choices":["Inspect only the symptomatic nail to avoid unnecessary examination","Defer examination until pigment has been present for a year","Perform a potassium hydroxide preparation before any inspection","Remove polish and inspect every nail","Obtain radiographs of all digits routinely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. History and examination should document onset, evolution, trauma, bleeding, pain or cold sensitivity, prior treatments, medications, immune status, human papillomavirus risk, and personal or family melanoma history, and polish must be removed so that every nail can be inspected. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A clinician evaluating a 58-year-old man for a condition that would be dangerous to miss wants a test that will correctly identify patients who do have the condition, minimising the number of affected patients missed. What property is she describing?","choices":["Posttest probability","Positive predictive value","Pretest probability","Specificity","Sensitivity"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Sensitivity is the probability that the test shows a person has the condition when they do have it — how well a test detects a condition. Sensitivity is related to fewer false negatives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm nodule on her lower leg with a brown halo and a slightly raised centre. When the skin on either side is squeezed together, the lesion dimples downward. What is this sign, and what does dermoscopy typically show?","choices":["Nikolsky sign","The dimple sign","Auspitz sign","Darier sign","The dimple sign, seen in lipoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The dimple sign is characteristic of dermatofibroma, which is a firm 0.5 to 1 cm nodule formed by dense clusters of dermal fibroblasts, most commonly on the legs. Dermoscopy often shows a peripheral pigment network with a central white area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has a warm, tender, erythematous area on the right lower leg with indistinct borders and surrounding oedema. What feature most reliably distinguishes cellulitis from erysipelas?","choices":["Only cellulitis follows a portal of entry","Only cellulitis produces fever","Only erysipelas is streptococcal","Cellulitis is bilateral, erysipelas unilateral","Cellulitis has indistinct, unraised borders"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Erysipelas involves the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, producing a plaque raised above the surrounding skin with a clear line of demarcation. Cellulitis involves the deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue and therefore has borders that are indistinct and not raised. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A clinician has settled quickly on a diagnosis for a 50-year-old man and is now gathering data that would support it. Which suggestion for good decision-making addresses confirmation bias most directly?","choices":["Gather all data regardless of relevance","Rely on quick pattern recognition","Defer decisions until all results return","Ask questions that would disprove it","Apply a guideline rule early"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Among the suggestions for good decision-making is to ask questions to disprove rather than confirm, alongside slowing down, being aware of base rates, considering what data is truly relevant, and actively seeking alternative diagnoses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has had urticarial lesions for two months. Individual lesions persist for more than 24 hours and resolve leaving brownish discoloration. She reports arthralgia. What should be suspected?","choices":["Urticarial vasculitis","Ordinary chronic urticaria","Anaphylaxis","Mastocytosis","Hereditary angioedema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Urticarial vasculitis is distinguished by lesions persisting beyond 24 hours that leave bruising, with low complement levels and a biopsy showing leukocytoclastic vasculitis. Ordinary wheals blanch fully and last under 24 hours. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is about to be started on carbamazepine for newly diagnosed epilepsy. Which human leukocyte antigen association should prompt caution before prescribing it?","choices":["Human leukocyte antigen B*15:02","Human leukocyte antigen DQ2","Human leukocyte antigen B27","Human leukocyte antigen Cw6","Human leukocyte antigen B*58:01"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Human leukocyte antigen B*15:02 is the risk allele associated with carbamazepine, while human leukocyte antigen B*58:01 is associated with allopurinol. Both are listed risk factors for Stevens-Johnson syndrome. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy is seen for a recurrent, intensely pruritic rash. Examination shows poorly demarcated erythematous plaques with excoriations and lichenification of the antecubital and popliteal fossae. His mother reports a history of asthma. There is no fever, drainage, crusting, or tenderness. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Topical steroid plus emollient","Systemic prednisone","Oral terbinafine","Emollients alone, without a steroid","Oral cephalexin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment combines barrier repair with inflammation control: low to medium potency topical corticosteroid for the body, applied sparingly, with an emollient applied after rinsing. Neither element alone is adequate, which is what makes this the complete answer. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man who worked outdoors for forty years has multiple pink papules 0.3 to 0.5 cm on the bald scalp and dorsal hands. Several are more apparent to the examining hand than to the eye. None is indurated, ulcerated, or bleeding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Actinic keratosis","Basal cell carcinoma","Verruca vulgaris","Seborrheic keratosis","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses are small 0.2 to 0.6 cm flesh-coloured, pink, or slightly hyperpigmented papules with a characteristic sandpaper texture on palpation, so a lesion may be more apparent by touch than by sight, on chronically sun-exposed skin. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 29-year-old fair-skinned woman asks how to use sunscreen correctly before a beach holiday. What advice matches the guidance given?","choices":["Physical barriers alone","Broad-spectrum factor 30 daily, applied once","Factor 30 daily, reapplied every 2 hours","Factor 15, reapplied only after swimming","Only for Fitzpatrick types I to III"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Broad-spectrum sun protection factor 30 or above is advised for daily use and 50 or above for prolonged outdoor exposure, applied 15 minutes before sun exposure and reapplied every 2 hours. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with obesity who smokes has recurrent painful nodules and draining lesions in both axillae and the groin, with five episodes in six months. Examination shows inflamed nodules and several sinus tracts. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hidradenitis suppurativa","Furunculosis","Acne vulgaris","Cutaneous Crohn disease","Carbuncle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hidradenitis suppurativa is inflammation of apocrine glands affecting the axilla, groin, breasts, or perineum, and its three diagnostic elements are typical lesions, characteristic distribution, and recurrence more than twice in six months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman of Mediterranean descent presents with painful mouth erosions that began three months ago, followed by skin lesions. Examination shows flaccid bullae and widespread crusted erosions on the scalp, chest, axillae, and groin that bleed easily and are painful. Lateral pressure on normal-appearing skin causes the superficial layers to slip away. What does the biopsy most likely demonstrate?","choices":["Acantholysis with intraepithelial blisters","Neutrophils in a row at the junction","Band-like lymphocytes in the dermis","Granular immunoglobulin A at papillary tips","Subepithelial blistering, no acantholysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pemphigus is characterised by loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion, termed acantholysis, producing intraepithelial blisters. The oral onset, flaccid easily ruptured bullae, and positive Nikolsky's sign all identify pemphigus, and biopsy demonstrating acantholysis is the diagnostic finding. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 132"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man reports a single 4 cm oval salmon-coloured scaly plaque on his back ten days ago, followed by a shower of smaller similar lesions across the trunk whose long axes run along the rib lines. He feels well. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Nummular eczema","Pityriasis rosea","Secondary syphilis","Guttate psoriasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A herald patch precedes the exanthem in 50 to 90 percent of cases: a 2 to 5 cm oval salmon-coloured plaque that clears centrally, followed 1 to 2 weeks later by smaller lesions in the Christmas tree pattern. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has several beige to dark brown papules and plaques 5 to 15 mm on the trunk that feel velvety and appear stuck onto the skin surface. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Malignant melanoma","Congenital melanocytic naevi","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans","Solar lentigines","Seborrheic keratoses"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic keratoses are benign papules and plaques, beige to brown to black, 2 to 20 mm in diameter, feeling velvety or warty and appearing stuck or pasted onto the skin. They are common in older adults and are easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl starting treatment for acne of the face and back asks how quickly it will work. What education is accurate?","choices":["Response cannot be measured objectively","Improvement within one week","4 to 6 weeks, with all sites responding equally","No improvement for at least six months","4 to 6 weeks; back and chest take 3 to 4 months"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Improvement in lesions can take 4 to 6 weeks, clinical improvement is measured by the number of new lesions after 6 to 8 weeks of therapy, and areas on the back and chest are slowest to respond, taking 3 to 4 months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman reports gradual hair thinning over several years. Examination shows diffuse thinning across the central and parietal scalp with preservation of the frontal hairline. There are no bald patches, no scaling, no scarring, and no exclamation point hairs. Several of her male relatives began balding in their twenties. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lichen planopilaris","Male-pattern hair loss","Tinea capitis","Female-pattern hair loss","Alopecia areata"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Female-pattern hair loss produces diffuse thinning of the central and parietal scalp without significant change to the frontal hairline. There is an increased frequency of balding among first-degree male relatives, which the family history here supplies. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 146"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man who has worked outdoors for forty years is examined. Several pink papules 0.3 to 0.5 cm are visible on the scalp, and running a hand across the skin reveals further lesions that could not be seen at all. What is the characteristic tactile finding?","choices":["A stuck-on quality","Boggy and fluctuant","Velvety texture","Pearly with telangiectasias","Sandpaper texture"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses are small 0.2 to 0.6 cm flesh-coloured, pink, or slightly hyperpigmented papules with a characteristic sandpaper texture on palpation, and a lesion may be more apparent by touch than by sight. The skin must therefore be palpated, not only inspected. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man presents in late summer with numerous well-defined hypopigmented macules 4 to 5 mm over the upper back and shoulders, some coalescing. They did not tan with the rest of his skin. Scraping the surface reveals fine scale. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Postinflammatory hypopigmentation","Pityriasis alba","Vitiligo","Pityriasis versicolor","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pityriasis versicolor is caused by overgrowth of lipid-dependent Malassezia and produces velvety tan, pink, or white scaling macules 4 to 5 mm on the neck, upper arms, trunk, and groin. Hypopigmentation reflects altered melanocyte function and reduced tanning. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 58-year-old Black woman has had a pigmented band in the great toenail for two years that was previously attributed to trauma. Where does the tumour most often arise?","choices":["The nail bed","The proximal nail fold","The matrix","The periungual skin","The hyponychium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nail unit melanoma is a rare acral melanoma arising most often in the matrix. It is not clearly ultraviolet driven and may occur in any skin tone, and delayed recognition contributes to worse outcomes. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 4-month-old has a large facial hemangioma that is beginning to obstruct the visual axis of one eye. What is first-line treatment?","choices":["Systemic corticosteroids as first-line therapy","Immediate surgical excision in all cases","Serial observation alone","Pulsed dye laser as the primary treatment","An oral beta-blocker such as propranolol"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Beta-blockers are first-line treatment, for example oral propranolol or timolol. Indications for treatment include cosmetic concern, functional involvement such as blocked vision, deep ulceration, and infection. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity has multiple soft growths in both axillae and on the neck. How is an acrochordon best described morphologically, and where do they characteristically occur?","choices":["A bright red friable papule that bleeds","A translucent papule over a finger joint","A hard conical keratotic projection","A soft pedunculated papilloma in areas of friction","A firm nodule with a keratin core on the sole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An acrochordon is a fibroepithelial pedunculated papilloma with a narrow stalk and broad tip, occurring in areas of friction — the neck, axilla, and groin. Incidence is increased in women and in obesity, and they vary from over 1 mm to as large as 10 mm. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"An 8-year-old girl has an itchy scalp, low-grade fever, occipital lymphadenopathy, and irritability. Wet combing recovers several moving 2 mm insects, and small oval structures are cemented to hair shafts near the scalp. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea capitis","Atopic dermatitis","Scabies","Seborrheic dermatitis","Pediculosis capitis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Head lice are blood-sucking wingless insects about 2 mm long, common in children aged 3 to 12, producing pruritus, low-grade fever, regional lymphadenopathy, and irritability. Live lice found on combing indicate active infestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has a biopsy-proven keratoacanthoma on the dorsal forearm. What is the standard of care, and what margin is used for elliptical excision?","choices":["Excision with 1 mm margins","Intralesional corticosteroid","Excision with 5 mm margins","Cryotherapy without histology","Observation alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The standard of care is to excise or destroy the tumour, preferred because of possible malignancy, with elliptical excision using 5 mm margins. Mohs surgery may be indicated for large or recurrent lesions or those in cosmetically sensitive locations. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has two round, sharply circumscribed, slightly erythematous scaly plaques on the forearm, each with an advancing scaly border and a clearer centre. Where should the specimen for potassium hydroxide microscopy be taken from?","choices":["Normal skin adjacent to the lesion","The active advancing border of the lesion","A plucked hair from within the plaque","The centre of the lesion, where the change began","A nail clipping from the same limb"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The potassium hydroxide specimen is taken from the active border, where the viable organism is concentrated. Culture is added for high clinical suspicion when the potassium hydroxide preparation is negative. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man of Northern European ancestry presents with an intensely pruritic, burning eruption of grouped small vesicles and papules symmetrically distributed over the elbows, knees, and buttocks. Most lesions are excoriated. He has autoimmune thyroid disease. What direct immunofluorescence finding is expected on perilesional skin?","choices":["Band-like lymphocytes in the upper dermis","Net-like intercellular immunoglobulin G","Granular immunoglobulin A in dermal papillae","No deposits; mites on scraping","Linear immunoglobulin G at the basement membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dermatitis herpetiformis is caused by immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase in genetically susceptible individuals consuming gluten. The immune complexes deposit in the dermal papillae, and perilesional direct immunofluorescence showing granular immunoglobulin A is the gold standard test. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has several intensely itchy, well-demarcated coin-shaped plaques 2 to 4 cm on both lower legs, with prominent scale and crusting. Each plaque is uniform throughout, with no clearing in the middle. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pityriasis rosea","Nummular eczema","Tinea corporis","Stasis dermatitis","Psoriasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nummular eczema produces intensely pruritic round coin-shaped plaques 1 to 10 cm, most often on the extremities, and the plaques are uniform in appearance without central clearing. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 9-year-old presents with a fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius for three days, headache, and now a rash that began on the ankles and wrists and is spreading centrally. What is the treatment, and does his age change it?","choices":["Withhold until serology returns","Doxycycline, used in children for this infection","Intravenous ceftriaxone for 14 days","Supportive care only","Amoxicillin; doxycycline is contraindicated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rocky Mountain spotted fever is treated with doxycycline in adults at 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days, the same in pregnant women, and in children at 2.2 mg per kilogram every 12 hours. The illness is life threatening if untreated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 43-year-old factory worker splashes a concentrated alkali solution on the forearm, while a colleague of the same age has had weeks of soreness from repeated soap exposure. How does the timing of an irritant reaction to a strong alkali differ from that of a reaction to a mild irritant such as soap?","choices":["Alkalis act in minutes; soaps over weeks","Alkalis act systemically; soaps locally","Both act only after weeks","Neither acts before 48 to 72 hours","Alkalis over weeks; soaps in minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms present differently depending on the exposure: mild irritants such as soaps may present with subacute symptoms that progress over weeks, while alkalis and acids may present minutes after exposure or delayed up to 24 hours or more. Concentrated agents can lead to chemical burns and necrosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl presents with fever and headache four days after a tick bite, and a rash has now appeared. What is the clinical triad of Rocky Mountain spotted fever, and how often is it present?","choices":["Fever, cough, and rash","Fever, headache, and rash, in nearly all","Fever, arthritis, and erythema migrans","Fever, headache, and rash, in about 60 percent","Rash, pruritus, and lymphadenopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The clinical triad is fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius, headache, and rash, present in only about 60 percent of patients. Fever presents in the first 3 days with rash following 2 to 4 days after fever onset. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man presents two days after clearing brush at a summer job. He reports intense itching. Examination shows erythematous papules, vesicles, and a few small bullae arranged in linear streaks across the forearms and one calf, with excoriation between them. Some lesions appear newer than others. He is afebrile and the rash does not cross the midline of the trunk or follow a single band. What feature of the eruption best supports the diagnosis?","choices":["The onset within two days of the exposure","The presence of excoriation between lesions","The presence of vesicles and bullae","The intensity of the pruritus","The linear arrangement of the lesions"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Urushiol sap from Toxicodendron species produces intensely pruritic vesicular lesions in linear distributions, because the streaks mark where the plant grazed the skin in passing. Lesions erupt within 4 to 96 hours and may appear in multiple stages, as they do here. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 24-year-old traveller returns from a rural area having walked barefoot on sandy soil. He has several erythematous papules on the feet that have enlarged over weeks into firm, somewhat translucent yellow nodules 4 to 10 mm across, some painful. What is the diagnosis and how is it confirmed?","choices":["Cutaneous larva migrans, confirmed by the serpentine trail","Tungiasis, confirmed by dermoscopy visualising ovoid eggs","Bedbug bites, confirmed by inspection of the sleeping area","Scabies, confirmed by skin scraping","Cercarial dermatitis, confirmed by history of freshwater exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tungiasis is infestation by penetration of the adult female flea into human skin to lay eggs. Solitary or multiple erythematous papules enlarge over weeks to 4 to 10 mm, sometimes forming a firm translucent nodule, and dermoscopy is used to visualise the ovoid eggs. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with alcohol use disorder has severe leg pain after a minor injury. Over hours the skin has changed from red-purple to blue-grey, bullae with thick purple fluid have appeared, and the area that was exquisitely tender is now numb. He is febrile and tachycardic. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Deep vein thrombosis","Pyoderma gangrenosum","Necrotizing fasciitis","Cellulitis","Erysipelas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Necrotizing fasciitis progresses rapidly with skin colour changing from red-purple to blue-grey, bullae containing thick pink or purple fluid, and loss of tenderness as superficial nerves are destroyed — an ominous sign. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man in Connecticut has an expanding erythematous oval lesion 9 cm across with central clearing and a darker punctate centre at the site of a recent tick bite. He has mild fatigue. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lyme disease","Granuloma annulare","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Tinea corporis","Cellulitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1 early localised Lyme disease is characterised by erythema migrans: a large expanding erythematous round or oval lesion over 5 cm with central clearing and often a darker punctate centre at the bite site, also called a bull's-eye. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has a moveable, round, firm nodule on the back with a visible central pore, which occasionally discharges foul-smelling material. It is currently red, warm, and tender. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Excise the capsule now while inflamed","Leave it alone permanently","Aspirate and discharge","Urgent wide excision","Delay excision; inject triamcinolone now"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. If the cyst is inflamed, excision should be postponed for a few weeks, inflammation reduced with an intralesional steroid injection of triamcinolone, and antibiotics given if needed. Surgical removal of the entire capsule is performed when the cyst is not inflamed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has dermatomal pain but no vesicular eruption. What is this presentation called?","choices":["Disseminated zoster","The pre-eruptive phase","Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Postherpetic neuralgia","Zoster sine herpete"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Some patients have pain without a vesicular eruption, which is termed zoster sine herpete. Others have vesicular eruptions without pain, although almost all patients have pain during the acute eruptive phase. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 7-year-old girl is being treated for tinea capitis and her school has asked whether she must stay at home. What advice about school attendance and household measures is correct?","choices":["All household contacts take oral antifungals","Stop treatment when itching improves","Stay out of school until the scalp is clear","No exclusion once treated; do not share hair items","No household measures are needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. School exclusion is generally unnecessary once effective treatment has started. Patients should avoid sharing personal hair items and should clean clippers, combs, brushes, bedding, and hats, and household contacts may use a sporicidal shampoo. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man presents with thick scaly plaques on both elbows and knees that have been present for years. Examination shows well-demarcated salmon-pink plaques with adherent silvery scale over the extensor elbows and knees, the sacrum, and the scalp. A linear plaque has developed along a surgical scar on his forearm. When scale is lifted from one plaque, pinpoint bleeding appears. What is this bleeding phenomenon called?","choices":["Auspitz sign","Nikolsky sign","Darier sign","Wickham striae","Koebner phenomenon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Auspitz sign is the appearance of bleeding when scale is removed from a psoriatic plaque, produced by the dilated capillaries lying immediately beneath a thinned suprapapillary epidermis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has symmetrical, well-defined, completely white non-scaly macules and patches on the dorsal hands and around the mouth. They fluoresce under a Wood's lamp. Her mother has autoimmune thyroid disease. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pityriasis alba","Leprosy","Vitiligo","Pityriasis versicolor","Postinflammatory hypopigmentation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vitiligo is an autoimmune disease causing depigmentation through T cell mediated destruction of melanocytes, producing asymptomatic white non-scaly macules and patches with distinct margins that fluoresce under a Wood's lamp, usually symmetrical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with tender pretibial nodules undergoes a deep incisional biopsy. What finding confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["A dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate with no organisms","Lobular panniculitis with vasculitis","Palisading granulomas with central necrobiosis and mucin","Full-thickness epidermal necrosis","Septal panniculitis without vasculitis, with Miescher's granulomas"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy shows septal panniculitis without vasculitis and Miescher's granulomas. A deep incisional biopsy is needed because the pathology lies in the subcutaneous fat. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"}]};
